# Testing Best Practices

This document outlines best practices for testing in our project, focusing on unit tests, integration tests, and end-to-end tests.

## Test Structure

- Organize tests to mirror the source code structure
- Use descriptive test names that explain the expected behavior
- Follow the Arrange-Act-Assert pattern
- Keep tests independent of each other
- Use setup and teardown functions for repeated test logic
- Group related tests with describe blocks

## Unit Testing

- Test individual functions and components in isolation
- Mock external dependencies using Jest mocks
- Use React Testing Library for component testing
- Focus on testing behavior, not implementation details
- Keep unit tests fast and focused
- Aim for high test coverage of business logic

## Integration Testing

- Test the interaction between multiple components or functions
- Use realistic test data that mimics production
- Mock external services but test real component interactions
- Focus on testing user workflows and feature behaviors
- Use proper setup and teardown for test data

## End-to-End Testing

- Use Playwright or Cypress for E2E testing
- Focus on critical user journeys
- Keep E2E tests minimal but comprehensive
- Implement proper test data management
- Use visual regression testing where appropriate
- Run E2E tests in CI/CD pipeline

## Mock Data

- Create reusable mock data factories
- Use realistic mock data that mirrors production
- Implement proper typing for mock data
- Keep mock data maintainable and organized
- Use tools like MSW for API mocking

## Testing Hooks and Context

- Test custom hooks with renderHook
- Test context providers with proper wrapper components
- Focus on the behavior exposed to components
- Test edge cases and error conditions
- Use proper async testing patterns

## Test Coverage

- Aim for high coverage of business logic
- Configure Jest for accurate coverage reporting
- Use coverage reports to identify untested code
- Don't chase 100% coverage at the expense of test quality
- Focus on meaningful tests rather than coverage percentages

## Performance

- Keep tests fast by proper mocking and isolation
- Use Jest's watch mode for development
- Implement test sharding for large test suites
- Configure proper timeouts for async tests
- Use snapshot testing judiciously
